div.p:
Immerman called the meeting to order at 10:20 a.m. He welcomed members of the
                        public and then asked for approval of the December 2012 meeting minutes,
                        which was received via unanimous consent.

list.item:
Sped up sampling, noting that a scientific sampling of each box was in
                            use (and is able to be used as the material has already previously been
                            extensively reviewed in the past);

Don McIlwain, Initial Processing and Declassification Division;
                                Marvin Russell, Initial Processing and Declassification
                                Division;

The need to make the DOE review process speedier and more
                                efficient. He urged Stein to do what he could to effect this;
                                and

The Committee had reviewed several Access Guides that morning;
                                these were expected to help future researchers as well as users of
                                the volumes.

div.p:
Zelikow then raised the issue of gaps in the existing historical record that
                        three CIA Directors had committed the Agency to redress. He recalled that,
                        in an earlier, sadder time, Foreign Relations had
                        been unable to fulfil its obligation to publish a complete the historical
                        record on these issues. He believed that more recent volumes indicated that
                        the series is on the road to recovery and there will no such gaps in the
                        future. He proceeded to flag the most important gaps: 

France and Italy in 1947–1948;

paramilitary infiltration and sabotage in Eastern Europe (Ukraine,
                                Albania, Poland, and Czechoslovakia), 1947–1951;

infiltration into China, especially in 1949;

covert operations in Korea, 1950–1953;

the Philippines, especially the election of Magsaysay;

Iran, 1953;

Egypt from 1956 through Nasser ;

Indonesia 1958; and

Tibet, 1959.
                         Zelikow suggested that the Foreign Relations
                        series for the 1960s was beginning to close the gap between what the
                        historical community expects and what it gets. Zelikow asked how these
                        issues would be dealt with when Foreign Relations
                        must move on to keep abreast of the 30-year line.

Slany said that there are hard choices to make: we can select to do major
                        operations or we can select to do everything from the end of World War II;
                        it’s a process of picking and choosing.
